% Section 2: Background (compressed)

\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}

\paragraph{IE Ontology Structure.}
\label{sec:bg:ontology}
An IE ontology $\mathcal{O} = (\mathcal{E}, \mathcal{R}, \sigma)$ defines entity types $\mathcal{E}$, relation types $\mathcal{R}$, and a mapping $\sigma: \mathcal{R} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{E})$ specifying valid (head type, tail type) pairs per relation.
A triple $(h, r, t)$ is \emph{type-valid} if $(\mathrm{type}(h), \mathrm{type}(t)) \in \sigma(r)$.
We define the \emph{constraint tightness} of $r$ as $1 - |\sigma(r)| / |\mathcal{E}|^2$: tighter constraints leave fewer valid options, increasing violation likelihood.

\paragraph{Grammar-Constrained Decoding.}
\label{sec:bg:gcd}
Grammar-constrained decoding restricts each token $y_t$ to a mask $M_g \in \{0, 1\}^{|V|}$ derived from a target grammar.
XGrammar~\citep{dong2025xgrammar} compiles JSON Schemas into pushdown automata for efficient enforcement of structural constraints; Outlines~\citep{willard2023outlines} and SGLang~\citep{zheng2024sglang} offer similar capabilities.
However, these methods are limited to context-free constraints and cannot enforce cross-field semantic rules (e.g., ``if $r$ = \textsc{Feature-of}, then $\mathrm{type}(h) \in \{\textsc{Method}, \textsc{OtherScientificTerm}\}$''), which depend on previously generated field values.

\paragraph{Problem Statement.}
\label{sec:bg:problem}
Given $\mathcal{O}$ and an autoregressive LM $\mathcal{M}$, we seek constraints $\mathcal{C}$ ensuring: (a)~\textbf{structural validity}, i.e., well-formed JSON with $\mathcal{E}$/$\mathcal{R}$ membership; and (b)~\textbf{type validity}, i.e., $(\mathrm{type}(h), \mathrm{type}(t)) \in \sigma(r)$ for every triple.
Condition (a) is context-free; (b) is context-sensitive but \emph{prefix-decidable}: at the point where a type token is generated, all information needed (relation type, entity registry) is available in the decoded prefix.
This requires that each entity type name maps to a unique first subword token (\S\ref{sec:semantic}, Implementation), a design assumption verified for all tested configurations.

\subsection{Grammar Layer}
\label{sec:grammar}

The grammar layer compiles $\mathcal{O}$ into a context-free grammar (CFG) enforced at every decoding step via logit masking.
This compilation proceeds in two stages.

\paragraph{Schema-to-grammar compilation.}
We generate a JSON Schema encoding the structure in Eq.~\eqref{eq:output-schema} with \texttt{enum} constraints restricting entity types to $\mathcal{E}$ and relation types to $\mathcal{R}$.
This schema is compiled into an EBNF grammar via XGrammar~\cite{dong2025xgrammar}, producing a pushdown automaton tracking JSON structure and enum membership.

\paragraph{Logit masking.}
At step $t$, the automaton computes grammar-valid tokens $G(t) \subseteq V$; all others receive logits $-\infty$.
This simultaneously enforces: (i)~structural validity (well-formed JSON), (ii)~relation vocabulary membership ($r_j \in \mathcal{R}$), and (iii)~entity type membership ($\tau_i \in \mathcal{E}$).
Domain/range constraints are \emph{not} enforced, as they depend on entity types within each triple; this gap is filled by the semantic layer.

\subsection{Semantic Layer}
\label{sec:semantic}

The semantic layer enforces domain/range constraints through a stateful logits processor maintaining an entity registry during generation.

\subsubsection{Entity Registry}
\label{sec:entity-registry}

As the model generates the entity list, the semantic layer builds a registry $\mathcal{D}: \text{name} \to \text{type}$ mapping each entity to its declared type.
This registry is populated incrementally: when the model completes an entry $\{n_i, \tau_i\}$, the pair $(n_i, \tau_i)$ is added to $\mathcal{D}$.
The registry is then consulted during relation generation to look up the declared types of head and tail entities.

\subsubsection{Position Detection}
\label{sec:position-detection}

At each step $t$, the processor pattern-matches the decoded prefix to identify the current field position; semantic masks activate only at type-bearing positions (\textsc{HeadType}, \textsc{TailType}).

\subsubsection{Dynamic Masking}
\label{sec:dynamic-masking}

At type-bearing positions, the processor computes a semantic mask $S(t) \subseteq V$ restricting generation to type-valid tokens.

\paragraph{Head type masking.}
At a \textsc{HeadType} position, the processor considers the entity registry $\mathcal{D}$ and the current relation type $r_j$ (if already generated).
If $h_j \in \mathcal{D}$ and $\mathcal{D}[h_j] \in \operatorname{dom}(r_j)$, the mask allows only that registered type: $S(t) = \{\operatorname{tok}(\mathcal{D}[h_j])\}$, ensuring consistency with both the entity list and domain constraints.
Otherwise, if $r_j$ is known, the mask falls back to all domain-valid types: $S(t) = \{\operatorname{tok}(\tau) : \tau \in \operatorname{dom}(r_j)\}$.
If $r_j$ is unavailable, no semantic mask is applied.

\paragraph{Tail type masking.}
At a \textsc{TailType} position, the head type $\tau^h_j$ is already determined from the decoded prefix.
The processor computes $\operatorname{range}(r_j, \tau^h_j)$, the valid tail types conditioned on the generated head type (\S\ref{sec:formulation}).
If $t_j \in \mathcal{D}$, the mask further restricts to the registered type when it belongs to this set.
This sequential check ($\tau^h \in \operatorname{dom}(r)$, then $\tau^t \in \operatorname{range}(r, \tau^h)$) achieves exact pair-based enforcement, strictly tighter than an independent range check $\tau^t \in \operatorname{range}(r)$.

\paragraph{Tokenizer-agnostic type masking.}
Each type name $\tau \in \mathcal{E}$ must be mapped to token IDs for logit masking.
A naive approach maps $\tau$ to its first subword token and sets disallowed IDs to $-\infty$, but this fails in two scenarios:
(1)~SentencePiece tokenizers produce context-dependent tokenizations where the same string yields different token IDs depending on preceding context (e.g., after a JSON delimiter), and
(2)~ontologies with prefix-shared type names (e.g., \textsc{Sen}, \textsc{Sid}, \textsc{Side} in WikiEvents) map to the same first token, making single-token disambiguation impossible.
We resolve both with \emph{continuation masking}: at compile time, we scan the full vocabulary to build a prefix map identifying, for each type name, all tokens whose decoded text is a valid prefix.
At decode time, the semantic mask allows any token that continues a partially-generated type name, disambiguating over multiple steps.
This is tokenizer-agnostic, handling byte-level BPE (Qwen, Llama) and SentencePiece (Mistral) without tokenizer-specific logic.

\subsubsection{Grammar-Semantic Interaction}
\label{sec:interaction}

The final mask is the conjunction of $G(t)$ and $S(t)$, permitting only tokens satisfying both layers.
This embodies an \emph{enabling dependency}: semantic lookup requires relation types to be valid members of $\mathcal{R}$, guaranteed only by the grammar layer; without it, semantic-only constraints reduce DR by only 24\%.
When $S(t) \cap G(t) = \emptyset$, the system falls back to grammar-only control (\emph{grammar-first fallback}), preserving structural validity at the cost of a potential type violation.
Formal analysis of composition, fallback behavior, and constraint tightness is in \S\ref{sec:composition}.


\subsection{Decoding Strategies}
\label{sec:strategies}

\paragraph{S1: Single-pass decoding.}
Both constraint layers are active throughout a single generation pass.
The entity registry is populated as entities are generated and immediately available for relation-level type checking.
This is the simpler and recommended strategy. It achieves strong constraint enforcement with higher relation F1 than the two-pass alternative.

\paragraph{S2: Two-pass decoding.}\label{sec:s2}
A first pass extracts entities under grammar constraints; extracted entities compile an input-dependent grammar for the second pass, which generates relations under both grammar and semantic constraints.
S1 achieves higher R-F1 at comparable DR (\S\ref{sec:scierc-results}) and is the primary configuration.

